{"author":{"id":"a66e0db0201c515c5090d5e52c036411dda9ba2f138110a926616a18a8ea0dd6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790682575,"nonce":"ICohBRDGhXuwh8RjKmjzU0GiWYk=","message":"Rayzor adapter generation is now derived from the shared xgpu declaration.\n\n`generate_rayzor` emits a runtime-neutral typed model, exact `rayzor::gpu` method descriptors, and runtime symbols. It generates ABI shims that widen xgpu `i32`/`f32` values to Rayzor's `i64`/`f64` plugin slots; this is required for wasm, where mismatched function signatures cannot rely on native register behavior. Generated enums implement an adapter-supplied `NativeEnum` contract.\n\nThe Rayzor runtime prerequisite is also implemented locally: plugins can create pending futures and resolve or reject them from native callbacks, with single-winner settlement and error allocation deferred to the language thread. The generated adapter model type-checks in isolation. The full xgpu workspace, Caribou GPU plugin tests, and Rayzor Future/plugin tests pass.\n\nThe remaining Rayzor work stays host-owned: Text/Bytes/error/root carriers, package-table integration, and migration of Rayzor compute's current pointer-shaped `GpuBuffer` to xgpu's shared handle identity. `@:shader`, lazy graphs, fusion, quantized kernels, and Metal/CUDA policy remain Rayzor extensions over that identity.","files":null}]}